import{_ as o,C as n,o as s,c as i,j as t,a as r,E as d,a2 as l}from"./chunks/framework.sc8Z_ZE3.js";const w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Sensitive content handling","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"security/sensitive-content.md","filePath":"security/sensitive-content.md"}'),c={name:"security/sensitive-content.md"};function h(u,e,p,f,g,m){const a=n("Badge");return s(),i("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"sensitive-content-handling",tabindex:"-1"},[r("Sensitive content handling "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#sensitive-content-handling","aria-label":'Permalink to "Sensitive content handling"'},"​")],-1)),d(a,{type:"built",text:"Built"}),e[1]||(e[1]=l(' Octryx AI examines what goes to a model for sensitive content and acts on it per content kind. A Workspace admin chooses the action for each kind; a fresh install starts with the defaults below.<p>The kinds: credentials, credential assignments, email addresses, internal IP addresses, IBANs and phone numbers.</p><p>There are two places where this happens. They share one setting per kind:</p><ul><li><strong>The platform&#39;s own model calls</strong>: the calls the platform makes to a model itself, for example knowledge answers and research or the review of a workflow run, and the tool results it hands to a model.</li><li><strong>The gateway of external coding agents</strong>: the requests of Claude Code and Codex, on the governed routes and on a Claude or ChatGPT seat. The same five actions apply here: a request is refused, masked, rerouted, or sent unchanged and recorded.</li></ul><h2 id="defaults" tabindex="-1">Defaults <a class="header-anchor" href="#defaults" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Defaults&quot;">​</a></h2><p>These apply on a fresh install, and in every Workspace that changes nothing.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Platform&#39;s own model calls</strong></td><td>Credentials, credential assignments and email addresses are masked, internal IP addresses are allowed and recorded, IBANs and phone numbers are not acted on</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gateway of external coding agents</strong></td><td>Credentials and credential assignments are masked, email addresses and internal IP addresses are sent as they are and recorded, IBANs and phone numbers are not acted on</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Command safety</strong></td><td>A command with credentials, credential assignments or internal IP addresses is sent for review</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The two model defaults differ only in email addresses: in code they are real data (commit authors, package metadata, test fixtures), so a coding agent gets them as they are.</p><h2 id="the-five-actions" tabindex="-1">The five actions <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-five-actions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The five actions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A Workspace admin chooses one per kind in <strong>Agents › Governance › Policy</strong>, card <strong>Sensitive content handling</strong>. The choice applies to the platform&#39;s own model calls and to the gateway alike:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Refuse</strong> (<code>refuse</code>)</td><td>The request is not sent. The caller gets <code>content_policy_denied</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mask</strong> (<code>mask</code>)</td><td>Each value is replaced by a numbered placeholder before the model sees it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reroute</strong> (<code>reroute</code>)</td><td>The request goes to the Workspace&#39;s governance alias.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mask and reroute</strong> (<code>mask_reroute</code>)</td><td>Masked, and sent to the governance alias.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Allow (recorded)</strong> (<code>report</code>)</td><td>The text goes to the model unchanged; the finding is recorded as an informational decision.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>An admin can also set a kind back to its shipped default. The card shows the action in effect and the shipped default per path where the paths differ, as <strong>Platform calls</strong> and <strong>Coding agent gateway routes</strong> (for example email addresses: masked on platform calls, allowed and recorded on the gateway routes). Before you save, it shows what the chosen action does at every place, and where a place acts differently (for example, a reply masks instead of rerouting). Below the kinds it lists the rules that always apply.</p><h3 id="the-governance-alias" tabindex="-1">The governance alias <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-governance-alias" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The governance alias&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each Workspace has one <strong>governance alias</strong>: the single governed model alias (an alias of an active model gateway) that every rerouting kind is sent to. Kinds carry no alias of their own.</p><ul><li>A kind can be set to reroute only while the Workspace has a governance alias (<code>content_handling_alias_required</code>).</li><li>The alias cannot be cleared while a kind still reroutes (<code>content_handling_alias_in_use</code>, naming the kinds); it can only be replaced by another governed alias.</li></ul><h3 id="where-reroute-does-not-apply" tabindex="-1">Where reroute does not apply <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-reroute-does-not-apply" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where reroute does not apply&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>A model&#39;s reply</strong> goes back to the user, not to another model. There reroute and mask and reroute mask instead. The call&#39;s decision record is request-side: it says, for the request sent, which action was requested and which was applied. The reply adds no per-kind entries; a refused reply only sets <code>reply_refused: true</code> on that same record.</li><li><strong>Codex on a ChatGPT seat</strong>: the seat serves only the person&#39;s own login, so there is no governed alias to send the request to. Reroute and mask and reroute refuse the request with <code>content_route_unavailable</code>.</li><li><strong>Commands</strong>: a command cannot be masked or rerouted. Mask sends it for review; refuse, reroute and mask and reroute deny it (reroute with <code>content_route_unavailable</code>).</li></ul><h2 id="on-the-gateway-of-external-coding-agents" tabindex="-1">On the gateway of external coding agents <a class="header-anchor" href="#on-the-gateway-of-external-coding-agents" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;On the gateway of external coding agents&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The gateway protects against <strong>accidental</strong> leakage by your own coding agent and against prompt injection arriving in its requests. It does not defend against a deliberately evasive client: Claude Code and Codex are your own programs and can reach any provider directly.</p><ul><li><strong>The Workspace&#39;s handling applies to every kind.</strong> Refuse, mask, reroute, mask and reroute, and allow (recorded) act on the gateway as on the platform&#39;s own model calls.</li><li><strong>Refuse acts on the newest turn.</strong> A kind set to refuse refuses the request when it is in the newest turn: your new message and the tool results it carries. Anywhere else in the request (earlier turns the client re-sends, tool definitions, the system prompt) it is masked instead, so the conversation can go on. A refused credential is answered with <code>secret_detected_in_prompt</code>, every other refused kind with <code>content_policy_denied</code>.</li><li><strong>Masking changes the body that is sent.</strong> The masked body is examined again before it is sent; if a kind to mask is still there, the request is refused and nothing is sent.</li><li><strong>Reroute sends the request to the governance alias.</strong> On a governed route the request goes to that alias. On a Claude seat it leaves your subscription and goes to the governance alias as a governed call. On a ChatGPT seat it is refused (<code>content_route_unavailable</code>). The rerouted request is examined again for its new target.</li><li><strong>Prompt injection is refused in the newest turn&#39;s content</strong>: your new message and the tool results it carries (Claude Code&#39;s <code>tool_result</code> blocks, Codex&#39;s tool-call outputs), with <code>content_policy_denied</code>. Like a refused credential, an injection in an earlier turn does not refuse later requests. It is not checked in tool definitions or in the client&#39;s system prompt.</li><li><strong>The Workspace&#39;s custom content policies</strong> are asked too. A policy that blocks refuses the request. In enforce mode, a policy that asks to mask or reroute has its way: the request is masked or sent to the policy&#39;s alias, and asked again. If that cannot satisfy the policy, the request is refused (<code>content_handling_gateway_refused</code>). In shadow mode the request is sent and what the policy would have done is recorded.</li><li><strong>Bodies that cannot be checked are refused:</strong> invalid JSON (<code>request_body_invalid</code>), a body over 16 MiB (<code>request_body_too_large</code>), or a check not finished within 10 seconds (<code>content_scan_unfinished</code>). The next request is examined again.</li></ul><p>A refusal names the decision record and says what to do next: remove the affected message or start a new conversation (Claude Code: <code>/rewind</code> or <code>/clear</code>; Codex: start a new session). A sent request with a kind that is allowed and recorded gets a notice naming the kinds and the record. A credential refusal names the kind of credential on the run&#39;s timeline.</p><h2 id="what-stays-fixed" tabindex="-1">What stays fixed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-stays-fixed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What stays fixed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>No setting changes these:</p><ul><li>Text the detectors could not finish examining is withheld from a platform call; on the gateway the request is refused.</li><li>Content of an unknown shape in a platform call is refused (<code>content_unreadable</code>); on the gateway, a body that is not valid JSON is refused (<code>request_body_invalid</code>).</li><li>Prompt injection is refused: in every field a platform call sends to a model, and on the gateway in the newest turn&#39;s content.</li><li>A request that still carries a kind to mask after masking is refused: nothing is sent.</li></ul><h2 id="who-can-change-it" tabindex="-1">Who can change it <a class="header-anchor" href="#who-can-change-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Who can change it&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Workspace admins and owners, and platform admins. Members see the action in effect for each kind, and whether it is the default. Every change of an action or of the governance alias is written to the audit log as <code>content_handling.changed</code>; see <a href="/docs/admin/governance">Governance</a>.</p><h2 id="known-limitations" tabindex="-1">Known limitations <a class="header-anchor" href="#known-limitations" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Known limitations&quot;">​</a></h2><p>See <a href="/docs/start/known-limitations#sensitive-content-handling">Known limitations</a>.</p><h2 id="reference-how-the-checks-work" tabindex="-1">Reference: how the checks work <a class="header-anchor" href="#reference-how-the-checks-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reference: how the checks work&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The exact behaviour, for admins and reviewers who need it.</p><h3 id="the-gateway-in-detail" tabindex="-1">The gateway in detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gateway-in-detail" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The gateway in detail&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The gateway covers the Anthropic route and the Claude Code seat, and the <code>/openai</code> Responses route and the Codex seat. A governed call&#39;s payload is built before the scan: the model replaced by the alias&#39; model group and, on the Responses route, the effective output cap. A seat call scans the client&#39;s body. What is sent is exactly the serialization of the body the gateway last examined: the payload itself, or its masked form after the second examination.</p><p>The threat model has three consequences: only a minimal shape check beyond JSON parsing (a JSON object with a non-empty <code>model</code>, and a <code>messages</code> list on the Anthropic routes or an <code>input</code> text or list on the Responses routes); opaque fields skipped by an exact key list without content validation (below); caller-supplied names and types are not verified.</p><ul><li><strong>What is scanned.</strong> The whole JSON payload as one text, minus the values under the opaque key list, plus the structured credential assignments of its parsed keys and values. A field whose name ends in a credential name (<code>password</code>, <code>db_password</code>, <code>access_token</code>, <code>client-secret</code>, …) assigning a literal value is a <code>credential_assignment</code> finding: in tool arguments, tool results and prompt data, a string that is itself serialized JSON included, the opaque list honoured. It is classified the same way for every action.</li><li><strong>The newest turn.</strong> On the Anthropic routes, the messages after the last assistant message. On the Responses routes, the input items after the last assistant item or tool call, or the whole <code>input</code> when it is a text. A refuse kind refuses only when it is found there.</li><li><strong>Masking.</strong> Every string value is masked where a kind to mask is found, a credential assignment&#39;s value whole. Serialized JSON inside a string is masked field by field and serialized again. Not changed: opaque values and the body&#39;s structure (<code>model</code>, <code>type</code>, <code>role</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>call_id</code>, <code>tool_use_id</code>, <code>stream</code>, <code>cache_control</code>). A kind set to refuse is masked outside the newest turn. The masked body is examined again within the same deadline; a kind to mask still found, or an examination not finished, refuses the request (<code>content_policy_denied</code>) and nothing is sent.</li><li><strong>Reroute.</strong> A rerouting kind on a request not already going to the governance alias sends it there: the alias is admitted on the route and the request is planned again, within the same deadline. A target that cannot be admitted, a missing governance alias, or a target that asks for yet another alias refuses the request with <code>content_route_unavailable</code>: a request is rerouted at most once.</li><li><strong>Prompt injection</strong> is checked in the newest turn&#39;s content, each part read as one text: its own text and its tool results (Anthropic <code>tool_result</code> blocks, Responses <code>function_call_output</code> and <code>*_call_output</code> items). It is not checked in tool definitions, in earlier turns or in the client&#39;s system text.</li><li><strong>Bodies that cannot be checked.</strong> Not valid JSON (a repeated key, a nesting deeper than 64, a non-finite number such as a <code>NaN</code> or <code>Infinity</code> literal or a number beyond the float range, invalid UTF-8), or without the minimal shape: <code>request_body_invalid</code> (400). Over 16 MiB: <code>request_body_too_large</code> (413). Not checked before the deadline, or too large or dense to check: <code>content_scan_unfinished</code> (400). Nothing unfinished is remembered: the next request is examined again.</li><li><strong>Deadline.</strong> 10 seconds from the moment the request handler is entered, the body read included. It bounds the content examination, from entry to the last scan: masking, the second examination of a masked or rerouted request, the structured fields and the bytes to send. A passed deadline is the fixed unfinished refusal, before any finding reaches a custom policy or a record. Admission, budget and commit steps after a finished examination are not content work and are not bounded by it.</li><li><strong>Custom content policies</strong> (Cedar) are asked over the whole body&#39;s findings as found, before masking. A blocking policy refuses with <code>content_policy_denied</code>; policies that cannot be evaluated refuse a request with findings they decide on (<code>content_policy_unavailable</code>). In enforce mode, an obligation to mask masks the body, and the policies are asked again on what is sent; an obligation to reroute sends the request to the policy&#39;s alias and plans it again there. An obligation that cannot be met refuses with <code>content_handling_gateway_refused</code>; on a ChatGPT seat a reroute refuses with <code>content_route_unavailable</code>. In shadow mode the request is sent and what the policy would have done is recorded.</li><li><strong>Tool-result policies.</strong> In enforce mode, a Workspace policy that keeps a tool&#39;s results out of a model refuses the whole request on the gateway (<code>content_policy_denied</code>, or <code>content_handling_gateway_refused</code> when the policy asks to mask the result).</li></ul><h4 id="decision-records-on-the-gateway" tabindex="-1">Decision records on the gateway <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision-records-on-the-gateway" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision records on the gateway&quot;">​</a></h4><p>A content decision record is written once, when the request ends, from its actual final outcome:</p><ul><li>for every request refused for a content reason: an unreadable, oversized or late body, a fixed rule, the content handling, a tool-result policy, or a custom policy. It is <code>refused</code>, with the reason and the stage (<code>body</code>, <code>content</code>, <code>tool_result_policy</code>, <code>cedar</code>);</li><li>for every request with acted-on findings (refused, masked, rerouted, or allowed and recorded), or with a custom policy&#39;s action.</li></ul><p>The record states, per kind found, the action configured (<code>requested</code>) and the action applied (<code>applied</code>), the kinds allowed and recorded (<code>informational</code>), the kinds whose action is a Workspace setting rather than the default (<code>workspace_setting</code>), the alias the request went to (<code>target</code>) and the outcome. It never claims an action that was not applied:</p><ul><li>A request that was sent is <code>forwarded</code>: each kind applied as configured, a kind set to refuse that was masked outside the newest turn applied as <code>mask</code>. This holds also when the upstream answered with an error status (<code>upstream_status</code>): the body crossed the transport, so it is never <code>refused</code>. A request counts as sent the moment the upstream&#39;s response headers arrive, before any error body is read.</li><li>A refused request applies <code>refuse</code> to every kind found.</li><li>A request with findings that was not sent for another reason (a budget, an undelivered send, a cancellation or failure) is <code>not_sent</code>, with that reason and stage, and applies <code>none</code>.</li><li>A prompt-injection refusal states the injection&#39;s count (<code>prompt_injection</code>); a refusal by a fixed rule names it (<code>fixed</code>: <code>unreadable</code>, <code>unfinished</code>, <code>prompt_injection</code>, <code>final_bytes</code>).</li><li>A custom policy&#39;s part is stated under <code>cedar</code>: its policies, its obligation and what was applied (<code>mask</code>, <code>reroute</code>, <code>refuse</code>), or in shadow mode what it would have done.</li><li>A refusal for a non-content reason with nothing found (run limits, admission, budget or cost, authentication, a model denial, loop protection), and a clean forward, write no content record.</li></ul><p>A refusal names the record and says what to do next. A sent request with a kind allowed and recorded carries the notice header <code>x-octryx-obligation</code> (<code>content_handling; reported=&lt;kinds&gt;; decision=&lt;record&gt;</code>) on every returned answer, upstream errors and token counts included. Masked and rerouted kinds are stated in the record, not in the header. A credential refusal names the kind of credential on the run&#39;s timeline. The Agents badge counts what the newest turn carries.</p><h3 id="the-opaque-key-list" tabindex="-1">The opaque key list <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-opaque-key-list" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The opaque key list&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The only values not read, by key, wherever they are, without validating their content. The same list applies on the gateway and on the platform&#39;s own calls. Masking never changes them.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Skipped when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>signature</code></td><td>its value is a string (a thinking block&#39;s signed state)</td></tr><tr><td><code>encrypted_content</code></td><td>its value is a string (encrypted reasoning, compaction or web-search state)</td></tr><tr><td><code>data</code></td><td>its value is a string in an object whose <code>type</code> is <code>base64</code> (a media source) or <code>redacted_thinking</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>image_url</code>, <code>url</code>, <code>file_data</code></td><td>its value is a <code>data:</code> URL (an inline image or file)</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="the-platform-s-own-model-calls-in-detail" tabindex="-1">The platform&#39;s own model calls in detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-platform-s-own-model-calls-in-detail" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The platform&#39;s own model calls in detail&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li><strong>One budget per call.</strong> Every provider-bound field is examined once within one budget for the whole call: size, number of parts, and a deadline from the call&#39;s entry to the last scan, the final check&#39;s serialization included. The size cap counts the full serialized bytes, opaque values included. The clock is read once more after all content work; a deadline passed during the examination is the fixed unfinished refusal. The custom policies&#39; scans of a governed call, an obligation&#39;s masking pass included, are charged to the same budget. Once it is spent, the call is refused unfinished (<code>content_scan_unfinished</code>, a notice without counts) before any later decision.</li><li><strong>Refuse and history.</strong> A kind set to refuse refuses new content (the newest turn, system text, message fields) and newly offered tool definitions. Found in older history or in a replayed tool call, it is masked instead: that text was checked when it was new.</li><li><strong>Prompt injection</strong> is refused in every field sent to the model, the newest user text included. An administrator-authored agent role (&quot;Act as …&quot;) is the one phrasing not refused there.</li><li><strong>Platform instructions.</strong> A platform instruction is sent unchecked only when its text is exactly a shipped seed text (by content hash). An edited or Workspace-authored instruction is checked like any authored text.</li><li><strong>Unreadable structure.</strong> A request whose structure cannot be read (messages that are not a list of objects, a nesting deeper than 64, text that is not valid Unicode, a role that is not a string, an unknown block) is refused with <code>content_unreadable</code> before anything copies or hashes it.</li></ul><h4 id="the-final-check" tabindex="-1">The final check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-final-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The final check&quot;">​</a></h4><p>Right before a provider driver sends, the final request object it hands to its transport or SDK is read once more as one JSON text, minus the opaque key list, with the same structured credential classification as the gateway. It never rewrites text. The only text not read is a field that is exactly a shipped seed text.</p><p>Prompt injection is not evaluated in a system carrier of the driver&#39;s request whose value is exactly an authored system text of the call, or exactly the driver&#39;s join of the call&#39;s system texts. A system carrier is the top-level <code>system</code> or <code>systemInstruction</code>, or a message of the top-level <code>messages</code> or <code>input</code> list whose <code>role</code> is <code>system</code> or <code>developer</code>. Nothing else is looked at for a role: the same text in any other field, or inside a longer value, is evaluated, and every other detector reads the authored field whole.</p><p>The call is refused and nothing is sent if:</p><ul><li>prompt injection, or a kind whose action must keep it out (refuse, mask, mask and reroute), is still there;</li><li>a rerouting kind is there while the call is not going to the Workspace&#39;s governance alias;</li><li>the call ran out of its budget.</li></ul><p>Right before the send, after the custom policies, a call that a rerouting kind sent to the governance alias must still be going there; a mismatch is refused with <code>content_route_unavailable</code>.</p><h4 id="the-record-of-a-platform-call" tabindex="-1">The record of a platform call <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-record-of-a-platform-call" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The record of a platform call&quot;">​</a></h4><p>The record is request-side only. It is written once, after the request was handed to the transport, from the final check&#39;s reading of the sent bytes:</p><ul><li>a kind present in them is recorded with its applied action, <code>report</code> or <code>reroute</code>;</li><li>a kind the examination of the input found and the sent bytes do not carry is recorded <code>mask</code>;</li><li>a report first seen in the sent bytes is included; a report this process already recorded for the same text is named <code>reported_before</code>, and the earlier record keeps counting it;</li><li>a refused call is recorded <code>refuse</code>, refusals by custom policies and by the final check included;</li><li>a call neither refused nor sent (the driver failed, or the call was cancelled before the hand-off) has no record.</li></ul><p>The reply is masked or refused as configured but leaves no per-kind evidence: a refused reply only sets <code>reply_refused: true</code> on the call&#39;s record, and a reply part whose examination does not finish is withheld. The conversation&#39;s badge is counted after the record.</p><h3 id="tool-results" tabindex="-1">Tool results <a class="header-anchor" href="#tool-results" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tool results&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Each released tool result has at most one decision record, written once from what was actually released: unchanged, masked, blocked, or kept by a coding agent&#39;s client that cannot take a masked output. A result released unchanged with nothing to state has none.</p><ul><li>Each kind&#39;s <code>applied</code> is read from the released result: <code>report</code> only for a reported kind it carries; <code>mask</code> only for a kind found and absent from it (a Workspace policy&#39;s mask); otherwise <code>none</code>, because it applies where the result next reaches a model. <code>informational</code> lists the kinds applied as <code>report</code>.</li><li>A blocked result is <code>refuse</code>; an unfinished examination is the fixed record without findings.</li><li>The record is written when the check returns the result to its caller; a check that fails or is cancelled before that writes nothing.</li><li>A result the client keeps (a built-in tool) is released as it came, with nothing claimed as blocked, masked or withheld; the launcher&#39;s notice says the output stays and the gateway checks it. A Workspace policy&#39;s part, and a withheld count, are stated only for a released result.</li></ul><p>The conversation&#39;s badge counts only what entered the context, after the record.</p><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/docs/security/redaction">Secret redaction</a>: secrets replaced with <code>[redacted]</code> before text is stored, shown or sent on.</li><li><a href="/docs/rules/policies">Policies and four-eyes</a>: policies, detectors and the <strong>Content security</strong> overview.</li><li><a href="/docs/security/data-classes">Data classes</a>: which models may see a Workspace&#39;s data at all.</li></ul>',61))])}const k=o(c,[["render",h]]);export{w as __pageData,k as default};
